#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dd6988a-86b8-5b56-bf3e-fc0df20e099b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Inequalities With Variables on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "9f887f78-3ba8-540d-af89-1c3166ee6c38" :question/math-academy-id "e-12970" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5x+13 \\ge 3x-7$ and sketch the solution on the number line." :question/worked-solution "We solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5x + 13 & \\ge 3x - 7 \\\\ 5x + 13 - 3x & \\ge 3x - 7 - 3x \\\\ 2x + 13 & \\ge - 7 \\\\ 2x + 13 - 13 & \\ge - 7 - 13 \\\\ 2x & \\ge - 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & \\ge - 20 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge \\frac{-20}{2} \\\\ x & \\ge - 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\ge -10\n$$\n\nOur solution can be expressed using a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04b9a164fbff3b0603dcbd2b3283ad44ba480af91ae9af3dbd4ddcfe086c0752.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "215dc4e3-bf30-538d-bc8c-d67d6a60e12b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities by Clearing a Rational Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "232b1ad9-6651-582b-b942-b36c7896806e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12971" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2-5y}{3} \\le 4$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 - 5y}{3} & \\le 4 \\\\ 3 \\cdot (\\frac{2 - 5y}{3}) & \\le 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 3 \\cdot (\\frac{2 - 5y}{3}) & \\le 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 2 - 5y & \\le 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 5y & \\le 12 \\\\ 2 - 5y - 2 & \\le 12 - 2 \\\\ -5y & \\le 10 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\ge\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\ge - 2 \\\\ y & \\ge - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny \\ge -2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9374b546-aa83-53f6-b38d-fc750350e659" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities With Rational Expressions and Variables on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "bab434ec-d18a-5d77-9bf1-8e16e9af2556" :question/math-academy-id "e-12974" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4y -1 > \\dfrac {5y - 2} {3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4y - 1 & > \\frac{5y - 2}{3} \\\\ 3 \\cdot (4y - 1) & > 3 \\cdot (\\frac{5y - 2}{3}) \\\\ 3 \\cdot (4y - 1) & > 3 \\cdot (\\frac{5y - 2}{3}) \\\\ 3 \\cdot (4y - 1) & > 5y - 2 \\\\ 12y - 3 & > 5y - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 12y-3 &> 5y-2 \\\\[5pt] 12y-3-5y &> 5y-2-5y \\\\[5pt] 7y-3 &> {-2} \\\\[5pt] 7y-3 +3 &> {-2} +3 \\\\[5pt] 7y &> {1} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7y & > 1 \\\\ \\frac{7y}{7} & > \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{7y}{7} & > \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ y & > \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny > \\dfrac17\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a00ac76c-0ebd-53cb-b689-98bda731064a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Inequalities Using Cross-Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "8740f864-62be-5391-bf06-ac4f4e1c1a2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12975" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3a-2}{5} > \\dfrac{4+6a}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by cross-multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3a - 2}{5} & > \\frac{4 + 6a}{2} \\\\ 2 \\cdot (3a - 2) & > 5 \\cdot (4 + 6a) \\\\ 6a - 4 & > 20 + 30a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6a - 4 & > 20 + 30a \\\\ 6a - 4 - 6a & > 20 + 30a - 6a \\\\ -4 & > 20 + 24a \\\\ -4 - 20 & > 20 + 24a - 20 \\\\ -24 & > 24a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & > 24a \\\\ \\frac{-24}{24} & > \\frac{24a}{24} \\\\ -1 & > \\frac{24a}{24} \\\\ -1 & > a \\\\ a & < - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a < -1$."}]}
